<section class="sg-sec sg-fam"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Songs that led to songs</h2><p>Tunes travel: an anthem borrows a hymn&#8217;s melody, a farewell song becomes a graduation song on the other side of the world. These are the links between the songs in this library; every song&#8217;s page lists many more.</p><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a><span class="sg-chain-h">same tune, Japanese words</span></li><li><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a><span class="sg-chain-h">named as inspired by it</span></li><li><a href="/songs/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a><span class="sg-chain-h">Pottier wrote the words to be sung to its tune</span></li><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/hawaii-ponoi">Hawaiʻi Ponoʻī</a><span class="sg-chain-h">melody reminiscent of it</span></li><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a><span class="sg-chain-h">the words may have been chosen for their likeness to it</span></li><li><a href="/songs/auld-lang-syne">Auld Lang Syne</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/hotaru-no-hikari">Hotaru no Hikari</a><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words</span></li><li><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a> <span class="sg-fam-arrow" aria-label="led to">&rarr;</span> <a href="/songs/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a><span class="sg-chain-h">opening motif inspired by it</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec sg-howto"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">How the songs are told</h2><p>The words of a song appear only where they are in the public domain: traditional and anonymous songs, and words whose authors died long ago or that were published before 1930. They are imported from the editions that hold them (Wikisource, Project Gutenberg, or the public-domain words quoted in Wikipedia&#8217;s articles), never typed from memory, and each names its edition. Where no older translation is free to use, the English and the romanization are the Institute&#8217;s own, made line by line from the original; notes say where a line can be read more than one way. Songs whose words are still in copyright have their story and their theme, told in our own words, but not their words.</p><p>Each performance was chosen by ear and by its source: the performers&#8217; own channels, choirs, ensembles and institutions, and old recordings. YouTube&#8217;s player loads only when you press play.</p></section>
